###Pure
// core-party -- legal entities, the identifiers they are known by, and how they own each
// other.
//
// The shape worth stating once, because every class below is a consequence of it: a legal
// entity has no single identifier. It has an LEI from GLEIF, a BIC from SWIFT, a tax number
// from each revenue authority it files with, and an internal number from whichever system
// onboarded it first. None of those is THE key, and any model that picks one of them as the
// key is a model that cannot record the second one. So identity here is always a pair --
// the entity, and the SCHEME the identifier was issued under -- and the composite primary
// key on CP_ENTITY_IDENTIFIER is that fact spelled in SQL rather than a normalisation trick.
//
// Layer 0: this project refers to nothing outside core_party::.

// The registered legal person: the thing that signs, owns and is sued.
//
// Deliberately NOT the counterparty, the client or the trading book -- those are roles a
// legal entity plays, they belong to whoever is modelling that role, and folding them in
// here is how a party master becomes unmaintainable. A dissolved entity stays in the table
// forever, because contracts it signed outlive it.
Class core_party::LegalEntity
{
   entityId: String[1];
   // The name on the incorporation certificate. Every other spelling -- transliterated,
   // trading, former -- lives in EntityName, because there can be many and this is the one
   // a court would recognise.
   legalName: String[1];
   entityCategory: String[1];
   incorporationDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   dissolutionDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   isActive: Boolean[1];
   isBranch: Boolean[1];

   // Dissolution is recorded by a date, not by clearing isActive: an entity can be inactive
   // for onboarding purposes and still legally exist.
   isDissolved() { $this.dissolutionDate->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// The legal system an entity is incorporated under, which is what decides its available
// legal forms, its register and its filing obligations.
//
// Coarser than a country on purpose: Delaware and Jersey are the entries that matter, and
// both are subdivisions rather than sovereign states, so the code is not an ISO country
// code even where it happens to look like one.
Class core_party::Jurisdiction
{
   jurisdictionCode: String[1];
   jurisdictionName: String[1];
   sovereignCode: String[1];
   companyRegisterName: String[0..1];
   isOffshoreCentre: Boolean[1];
}

// A form of incorporation -- GmbH, Ltd, Inc, KK -- and what it implies about liability.
//
// Keyed by jurisdiction as well as by code, because form codes are only unique inside a
// legal system: "SA" is a societe anonyme in France and a sociedad anonima in Spain, and
// they are not the same instrument.
Class core_party::LegalForm
{
   jurisdictionCode: String[1];
   formCode: String[1];
   formName: String[1];
   hasLimitedLiability: Boolean[1];
   canBeListed: Boolean[1];
}

// A company register, tax office or regulator that an entity is on the books of.
//
// This is the body, not the identifier it hands out -- Companies House is the authority,
// the company number is the identifier, and separating them is what lets one entity hold
// registrations in six countries without six columns.
Class core_party::RegistrationAuthority
{
   authorityId: String[1];
   authorityName: String[1];
   jurisdictionCode: String[1];
   authorityKind: String[1];
   registerUrl: String[0..1];
}

// A namespace that identifiers are issued in, and the rules for reading one.
//
// The scheme is what makes an identifier comparable: two strings are the same identifier
// only if they came from the same scheme, so this class is half of every key in the
// project. isUniquePerEntity is the flag that matters -- an entity has one LEI but many
// tax numbers, and the composite key permits both because it constrains neither.
Class core_party::IdentifierScheme
{
   schemeCode: String[1];
   schemeName: String[1];
   issuingBody: String[0..1];
   isGlobal: Boolean[1];
   isUniquePerEntity: Boolean[1];
   expectedLength: Integer[0..1];
   checksumRule: String[0..1];
}

// One identifier an entity is known by, in one scheme. The composite-key class.
//
// (entityId, schemeCode) and nothing shorter: entityId alone loses every identifier but
// one, and identifierValue alone collides across schemes -- a BIC and a local tax number
// can be the same eight characters. The value is NOT the key precisely because it changes:
// an LEI is re-issued, a tax number is superseded, and the row survives both.
Class core_party::EntityIdentifier
{
   entityId: String[1];
   schemeCode: String[1];
   identifierValue: String[1];
   status: String[1];
   isPreferred: Boolean[1];
   issuedDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   lastVerifiedDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   supersededValue: String[0..1];
}

// A name the entity is written as, other than in one canonical spelling.
//
// Keyed by (entity, nameType) because the interesting question is always "what is its
// trading name" rather than "list its names": one slot per type, and a rename overwrites
// rather than appends.
Class core_party::EntityName
{
   entityId: String[1];
   nameType: String[1];
   nameValue: String[1];
   languageCode: String[0..1];
   validFrom: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// A postal address the entity holds in one capacity.
//
// The registered address is a legal fact -- it is where service of process lands -- and is
// frequently a law firm rather than anywhere the business sits, which is why it must not be
// collapsed with the headquarters address.
Class core_party::EntityAddress
{
   entityId: String[1];
   addressType: String[1];
   addressLine: String[1];
   city: String[0..1];
   postalCode: String[0..1];
   countryCode: String[1];
}

// The entity's standing on one authority's register.
//
// Keyed by (entity, authority): a company is on its home register once, and a branch is on
// the host register too, so the same entity legitimately holds several registration numbers
// that are all current at once.
Class core_party::EntityRegistration
{
   entityId: String[1];
   authorityId: String[1];
   registrationNumber: String[1];
   registrationStatus: String[1];
   registeredOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   lastFilingDate: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// One edge of the ownership graph: who owns whom, how much, and under what reading.
//
// Type is part of the key, not a detail on it. The same pair of entities is simultaneously
// a direct-parent edge and an ultimate-parent edge when there is nothing in between, and
// the accounting and regulatory readings of the same shareholding routinely disagree -- so
// (parent, child, type) is the grain, and dropping type silently merges answers that were
// never the same answer.
Class core_party::EntityRelationship
{
   parentEntityId: String[1];
   childEntityId: String[1];
   relationshipType: String[1];
   ownershipPercentage: Float[0..1];
   isConsolidated: Boolean[1];
   accountingStandard: String[0..1];
   startDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   endDate: StrictDate[0..1];

   // An edge with no end date is still in force. Ownership history is kept, so the absence
   // of the end date is the live-ness test rather than the row's presence.
   isCurrent() { $this.endDate->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// A set of entities treated as one for some purpose -- consolidation, credit, reporting.
//
// A group is not the top of the ownership tree. It is a decision about which subset of the
// tree gets aggregated, and different functions draw the line in different places over the
// same shareholdings, so groups are enumerated rather than derived from relationships.
Class core_party::EntityGroup
{
   groupId: String[1];
   groupName: String[1];
   groupType: String[1];
   headEntityId: String[0..1];
   asOfDate: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// One entity's place in one group.
//
// Keyed by (group, entity): the same entity sits in an accounting group and a credit group
// with different roles and different consolidation treatment, and neither key half is
// droppable.
Class core_party::GroupMembership
{
   groupId: String[1];
   entityId: String[1];
   memberRole: String[1];
   isFullyConsolidated: Boolean[1];
   includedFrom: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// What an entity does, according to one taxonomy.
//
// Keyed by (entity, taxonomy) because NACE, SIC and the MiFID counterparty categories
// answer different questions and are maintained by different people; the reconciliation
// between them is a downstream problem and is not attempted here.
Class core_party::EntityClassification
{
   entityId: String[1];
   taxonomyCode: String[1];
   classificationCode: String[1];
   classificationLabel: String[0..1];
   isPrimary: Boolean[1];
   assignedDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   assignedBy: String[0..1];
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------- associations
// Navigation, not containment. Every end below is reachable by a join on the store, so a
// downstream project can walk entity -> identifier -> scheme without repeating the keys.

// An entity to everything it is called in a scheme. The to-many end is the point: any
// model where this is [0..1] has already lost an identifier.
Association core_party::EntityIdentifiers
{
   identifiedEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   identifiers: core_party::EntityIdentifier[*];
}

// The other half of the composite key, navigable. "Every LEI we hold" is a scheme-side
// question, and it is asked more often than the entity-side one.
Association core_party::SchemeIdentifiers
{
   scheme: core_party::IdentifierScheme[1];
   issuedIdentifiers: core_party::EntityIdentifier[*];
}

Association core_party::EntityNames
{
   namedEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   names: core_party::EntityName[*];
}

Association core_party::EntityAddresses
{
   addressedEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   addresses: core_party::EntityAddress[*];
}

Association core_party::EntityClassifications
{
   classifiedEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   classifications: core_party::EntityClassification[*];
}

Association core_party::EntityRegistrations
{
   registeredEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   registrations: core_party::EntityRegistration[*];
}

Association core_party::AuthorityRegistrations
{
   authority: core_party::RegistrationAuthority[1];
   authorityRegistrations: core_party::EntityRegistration[*];
}

// The downward half of the hierarchy: an entity to the edges where it is the PARENT.
// Separate from the upward half because the same table is reached by a different column,
// and one association cannot be two joins.
Association core_party::ParentEdges
{
   parentEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   subsidiaryEdges: core_party::EntityRelationship[*];
}

// The upward half. To-many, not [0..1]: an entity has one direct parent but several
// ultimate parents once regulatory and accounting readings are both recorded.
Association core_party::ChildEdges
{
   childEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   parentEdges: core_party::EntityRelationship[*];
}

Association core_party::GroupMemberships
{
   group: core_party::EntityGroup[1];
   memberships: core_party::GroupMembership[*];
}

Association core_party::EntityMemberships
{
   memberEntity: core_party::LegalEntity[1];
   entityMemberships: core_party::GroupMembership[*];
}

Association core_party::EntityIncorporation
{
   jurisdiction: core_party::Jurisdiction[0..1];
   incorporatedEntities: core_party::LegalEntity[*];
}

// Reached by a TWO-column join, since a legal form is only identified with its
// jurisdiction. The join is where that composite key stops being a comment.
Association core_party::EntityForm
{
   legalForm: core_party::LegalForm[0..1];
   entitiesOfForm: core_party::LegalEntity[*];
}

Association core_party::JurisdictionForms
{
   formJurisdiction: core_party::Jurisdiction[1];
   legalForms: core_party::LegalForm[*];
}

Association core_party::JurisdictionAuthorities
{
   authorityJurisdiction: core_party::Jurisdiction[1];
   authorities: core_party::RegistrationAuthority[*];
}
